{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates whether either lottery contains a 'sure outcome'—that is, an outcome that occurs with 100% probability. In this decision problem, neither Lottery A nor Lottery B has a sure outcome. Lottery A yields -3000 Euros with 0.2% probability and 0 Euros with 99.8%, while Lottery B yields -6000 Euros with 0.1% and 0 Euros with 99.9%. Since both lotteries involve uncertainty (no outcome has 100% probability), neither contains a sure outcome. Therefore, according to the decision reason, both lotteries would be considered equally non-sure, and the reason would either be indifferent or prefer one without a sure outcome—which in this case applies to both. Thus, the decision reason can be applied, and its relevant information is that both lotteries lack a sure outcome.",
